fof_version: '0.1'
type: enemy
id: veteran_soldier
name: Veteran Soldier

ruleset:
  modules:
  - id: base
    version: 0.1.0

enemy:
  tier: named
  resolve: 4
  attack_modifier: 3    # Strength +2, Combat Practiced +1
  armor: light
  techniques: [telegraphed_finisher]
  # Telegraphed Finisher (MM1, Three Worked Enemy Techniques): once per
  # scene, against a character already carrying a Tier 2 Condition, its
  # attack repeats that Condition's type — a landed repeat is Broken.
  # Named one full exchange before it can land. Always.
  special: null
  tr: 11                # offense(+3→5) + durability(Resolve 4) + armor(light→1) + techniques(1) = 11

  disposition: >
    Trained, unhurried, and entirely without anything to prove.

  morale: >
    Withdraws in good order when a fight stops being worth the wound.
    Does not rout.

  description: >
    A hardened fighter with years of real combat experience. Not a
    tactician — a survivor. Knows how to absorb punishment, how to
    read an opponent's weight shift, and exactly how long to wait
    before committing. Fights economically: no wasted motion, no
    bravado. Will push Aggressive only when the opponent is already
    compromised. Difficult to rattle, difficult to put down.

  tactics: >
    Posture: Measured by default. Goes Aggressive if a party member
    is Cornered or Staggered. Goes Defensive from the exchange it is
    first left Open onward.
    Defends by meeting force with the blade, not by giving ground —
    while set (Measured or Defensive), lean toward Hard difficulty
    for Strikes against them (Chapter III.3). Targets the fullest-Endurance-Pool
    party member first to neutralize the biggest threat; shifts to
    a Cornered/Staggered party member to press the advantage — and
    that is when the Telegraphed Finisher comes out: the measured
    step, the blade drawn back, the whole table given one exchange
    to answer it.

  notes: >
    TR breakdown: offense(+3 → value 5) + durability(Resolve 4)
    + armor(light → 1) + techniques(1) = 11. (Was 10; gained the
    Telegraphed Finisher when MM1's worked enemy Techniques landed.)
    Actor count drives difficulty, not total TR (MM1, Encounter Recipe
    Table): a single Named enemy, however high its TR, is trivial for
    a fresh party. Use Veteran Soldier as part of a multi-actor roster
    to reach Standard or Hard difficulty, not alone.

created_at: '2026-03-05T00:00:00+00:00'
last_modified: '2026-08-08T00:00:00+00:00'
